.\" Copyright (c) 2026 NFS-Ganesha / ntirpc contributors
.Dd November 18, 2026
.Dt RPC_SVC_ZEROCOPY 3
.Os ntirpc
.Sh NAME
.Nm rpc_svc_zerocopy ,
.Nm svc_init ,
.Nm svc_set_socket_zerocopy ,
.Nm svc_get_socket_zerocopy_stats ,
.Nm svc_reset_socket_zerocopy_stats
.Nd TCP transmit zerocopy for large RPC replies
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libntirpc
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In rpc/rpc.h
.Ft bool
.Fn svc_init "svc_init_params *params"
.Ft void
.Fn svc_set_socket_zerocopy "bool enable"
.Ft void
.Fn svc_get_socket_zerocopy_stats "struct svc_socket_zerocopy_stats *out"
.Ft void
.Fn svc_reset_socket_zerocopy_stats "void"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
On Linux, ntirpc can send large connection-oriented (TCP) RPC replies with
.Dv MSG_ZEROCOPY
after
.Dv SO_ZEROCOPY
is set on the accepted socket.
Eligible sends avoid copying userspace payload into kernel skbuffs.
The kernel pins the iovec pages until it posts a completion on the socket
error queue
.Pq Dv MSG_ERRQUEUE ,
surfaced as
.Dv EPOLLERR
/
.Dv POLLERR
(not ordinary
.Dv EPOLLIN
/
.Dv EPOLLOUT ) .
ntirpc drains those completions and only then destroys the reply XDR,
including gifted
.Pq Dv UIO_FLAG_REFER
buffers.
.Pp
The feature is optional and off by default.
Compile-time support is advertised as
.Dv SVC_PARAM_HAS_TCP_ZEROCOPY
when the platform provides
.Dv SO_ZEROCOPY
and
.Dv MSG_ZEROCOPY .
If
.Fn setsockopt
fails at accept time, that transport falls back to classic
.Fn sendmsg
for its lifetime.
.Ss Enabling via svc_init
Configure fields in
.Vt svc_init_params
before calling
.Fn svc_init .
Two parallel name sets exist; either may be used:
.Bl -tag -width tcp_zerocopy_max_pendingXX
.It Va tcp_zerocopy_enabled / Va socket_zerocopy
When true, call
.Dv setsockopt(SO_ZEROCOPY)
on newly accepted TCP sockets and allow
.Dv MSG_ZEROCOPY
on eligible sends.
Default: false.
Prefer
.Va tcp_zerocopy_*
in new ntirpc code;
.Va socket_zerocopy*
aliases are kept for NFS-Ganesha / FSAL callers.
.It Va tcp_zerocopy_min_bytes / Va socket_zerocopy_threshold
Minimum reply size in bytes before attempting
.Dv MSG_ZEROCOPY .
Zero selects the default (16 KiB).
The value is rounded up to a page boundary.
Gifted
.Dv UIO_FLAG_REFER
payloads (for example Ceph READ pages) use this threshold as-is;
other payloads use twice the threshold so small non-refer replies stay on the
classic path.
.It Va tcp_zerocopy_max_pending / Va socket_zerocopy_max_pending
Cap on outstanding uncompleted zerocopy payload bytes per transport.
Zero selects the default (64 MiB).
Sends that would exceed the cap fall back to classic
.Fn sendmsg
and increment the
.Va fallback
counter.
.El
.Pp
Example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
svc_init_params p = { 0 };

p.flags = SVC_INIT_EPOLL;
#ifdef SVC_PARAM_HAS_TCP_ZEROCOPY
p.tcp_zerocopy_enabled = true;
p.tcp_zerocopy_min_bytes = 16384;       /* 0 = default 16 KiB */
p.tcp_zerocopy_max_pending = 0;         /* 0 = default 64 MiB */
/* Or Ganesha-style aliases:
 * p.socket_zerocopy = true;
 * p.socket_zerocopy_threshold = 16384;
 * p.socket_zerocopy_max_pending = 0;
 */
#endif
if (!svc_init(&p))
	err(1, "svc_init");
.Ed
.Ss Runtime control
.Fn svc_set_socket_zerocopy
toggles the feature after init.
It updates the process-wide enable flag used by subsequent eligibility checks
and by accept-time
.Dv SO_ZEROCOPY
setup.
Sockets already accepted keep their existing
.Va zc_sock_ok
state until closed; disabling stops new
.Dv MSG_ZEROCOPY
sends even on sockets that still have the option set.
.Ss When MSG_ZEROCOPY is used
For each TCP reply flush chunk, ntirpc selects
.Dv MSG_ZEROCOPY
only when all of the following hold:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Va tcp_zerocopy_enabled
is true.
.It
Accept-time
.Dv SO_ZEROCOPY
succeeded
.Pq Va zc_sock_ok
on that transport.
.It
Remaining bytes meet the REFER-aware threshold described above.
.It
Outstanding zerocopy bytes plus this send fit under
.Va tcp_zerocopy_max_pending .
.It
The send is not going through an established TLS path
(TLS encrypts into its own buffers; cleartext
.Dv MSG_ZEROCOPY
does not apply).
.El
.Pp
.Dv ENOBUFS
on a zerocopy attempt retries that chunk once without
.Dv MSG_ZEROCOPY
and counts
.Va enobufs
/
.Va fallback .
.Ss Completions and buffer lifetime
Successful
.Dv MSG_ZEROCOPY
sends assign per-socket cookies tracked on the reply ioq.
The ioq (and any
.Dv UIO_FLAG_REFER
pages it holds) stays alive until the kernel reports completion via
.Dv MSG_ERRQUEUE .
Callers that gift buffers into the XDR ioq must not free them until ntirpc
destroys that ioq after completion.
.Pp
Loopback and some paths may complete with
.Dv SO_EE_CODE_ZEROCOPY_COPIED
(kernel deferred copy).
After the first such notice on a transport, ntirpc clears
.Va zc_sock_ok
so later sends on that socket omit
.Dv MSG_ZEROCOPY ;
in-flight completions are still drained.
.Pp
.Dv EPOLLERR
with no active zerocopy interest
.Pq Va zc_sock_ok
false and no in-flight / pending ZC
is treated as a real socket error: the transport is destroyed instead of
rearmed, to avoid an event storm.
.Ss Statistics
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct svc_socket_zerocopy_stats {
	bool enabled;
	u_int threshold;
	u_int max_pending;
	uint64_t sends;
	uint64_t completions;
	uint64_t copied;    /* SO_EE_CODE_ZEROCOPY_COPIED */
	uint64_t enobufs;
	uint64_t fallback;
};
.Ed
.Pp
.Fn svc_get_socket_zerocopy_stats
fills a best-effort atomic snapshot of the counters and the effective
threshold / max-pending.
.Fn svc_reset_socket_zerocopy_stats
clears the counters only
.Pq not the enable / threshold / max-pending settings .
On platforms without zerocopy support the getters zero the structure and the
setters are no-ops.
.Ss NFS-Ganesha configuration
When used under NFS-Ganesha, the same knobs are typically mapped from
.Va NFS_Core_Param
into
.Vt svc_init_params
via the
.Va socket_zerocopy*
aliases:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
NFS_Core_Param {
	Enable_Socket_Zerocopy = true;
	Socket_Zerocopy_Threshold = 16384;   /* 0 = ntirpc default */
	Socket_Zerocopy_Max_Pending = 0;     /* 0 = ntirpc default */
}
.Ed
.Ss Debugging
Enable the
.Dv TIRPC_DEBUG_FLAG_ZEROCOPY_TX
debug flag to log
.Dv SO_ZEROCOPY
setup,
.Dv MSG_ZEROCOPY
send decisions, completions, and copied / fallback events.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn svc_init
returns
.Dv true
on success,
.Dv false
on failure.
.Fn svc_set_socket_zerocopy ,
.Fn svc_get_socket_zerocopy_stats ,
and
.Fn svc_reset_socket_zerocopy_stats
return no value.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sendmsg 2 ,
.Xr setsockopt 2 ,
.Xr rpc 3 ,
.Xr rpc_svc_calls 3 ,
.Xr rpc_svc_create 3
.Pp
Linux kernel documentation:
.Lk https://docs.kernel.org/networking/msg_zerocopy.html MSG_ZEROCOPY
.Sh HISTORY
TCP reply zerocopy support was added to ntirpc for large
.Fn svc_vc_create
sends with deferred buffer release on ERRQUEUE completion,
per-transport pending-byte caps, and REFER-aware size thresholds.
